      "completion_tokens": 1156,
      "output": "**Water** is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's hydrosphere and the fluids of all known living organisms. It is essential for all known forms of life.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is from chemical, physical, biological, and planetary perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Chemical Identity\n*   **Molecular Formula:** **H₂O** (two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom).\n*   **Molecular Structure:** The molecule is **bent (V-shaped)** at an angle of ~104.5°. Because oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, the molecule is **polar**—it has a partial negative charge near the oxygen and partial positive charges near the hydrogens.\n*   **Hydrogen Bonding:** This polarity allows water molecules to stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion) via **hydrogen bonds**. This relatively weak intermolecular force is responsible for almost all of water’s unique \"anomalous\" properties.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Physical States (Phase Transitions)\nWater is the only common substance to exist naturally on Earth in all three states:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Freezes at **0°C (32°F)**. Unusually, ice is **less dense** than liquid water (it expands ~9% upon freezing), causing it to float. This insulates bodies of water, allowing life to survive underneath.\n*   **Liquid (Water):** Exists between 0°C and 100°C (at 1 atm pressure). It has a very high **surface tension** and **specific heat capacity**.\n*   **Gas (Water Vapor/Steam):** Boils at **100°C (212°F)** (at 1 atm). It is a potent greenhouse gas.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key \"Anomalous\" Properties (Caused by Hydrogen Bonding)\n| Property | Significance |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Universal Solvent** | Dissolves more substances than any other liquid (salts, sugars, gases, acids), enabling chemical transport in biology and geology. |\n| **High Specific Heat** | Absorbs huge amounts of heat with little temperature change. Stabilizes Earth's climate and organism body temperatures. |\n| **High Heat of Vaporization** | Evaporation cools surfaces efficiently (sweating, transpiration in plants). |\n| **Density Maximum at 4°C** | Water is densest at 4°C, not at freezing. Lakes freeze from the top down. |\n| **High Surface Tension** | Allows capillary action (water moving up plant roots/stems) and supports small insects (water striders). |\n| **Amphoteric Nature** | Acts as both an acid (donates H⁺) and a base (accepts H⁺), central to pH balance. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. Planetary Context (The Hydrosphere)\n*   **Coverage:** ~71% of Earth's surface.\n*   **Distribution:**\n    *   **97.5% Saline** (Oceans).\n    *   **2.5% Freshwater** (mostly locked in ice caps/glaciers ~68.7%, groundwater ~30.1%, surface water/lakes/rivers ~0.3%).\n*   **The Water Cycle:** Continuous movement via evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation, and runoff. It drives weather and climate.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Biological Role\n*   **Medium of Life:** The solvent for all biochemical reactions (metabolism, DNA replication, protein folding).\n*   **Transport:** Blood (animals) and sap (plants) are water-based transport systems for nutrients and waste.\n*   **Structure:** Provides turgor pressure in plant cells (rigidity) and hydrostatic skeletons in worms/jellyfish.\n*   **Reactant:** Essential for **hydrolysis** (breaking down polymers) and **photosynthesis** (6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂).\n*   **Thermoregulation:** High heat capacity buffers internal temperature; evaporation cools.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Human Use & Challenges\n*   **Direct Consumption:** Drinking, cooking, sanitation (WHO recommends 50–100L/person/day for basic needs).\n*   **Agriculture:** ~70% of global freshwater withdrawals (irrigation).\n*   **Industry:** ~19% (cooling, processing, manufacturing, energy production).\n*   **Scarcity & Pollution:** Physical scarcity (arid regions), economic scarcity (lack of infrastructure), and contamination (industrial, agricultural, microbial) are major global crises.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Definition\n> **Water (H₂O) is a small, polar molecule whose hydrogen-bonding network creates emergent physical properties—floating ice, high heat capacity, and unparalleled solvency—that make it the thermodynamic and chemical foundation of planetary habitability and biological life.**"
